<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The three dynamic segments">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The directory is named</th><th>It matches</th><th>In <code>route.params</code></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>[id]</code></td><td>exactly one segment, required</td><td>a required key</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>[[tab]]</code></td><td>one segment or none</td><td>an optional key, absent when it did not match</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>[...rest]</code></td><td>the remaining segments, terminal</td><td>a list, empty when it matched nothing</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A rest segment is terminal because there is nothing a segment after it could match
        against. The other two compose freely, so <code>invoices/[id]/[[tab]]</code> is one route
        answering two addresses rather than two routes agreeing about an invoice.</p>

        <p>An optional segment that did not match is <strong>absent</strong> rather than empty. That
        is the difference between <code>{'const { tab = \'summary\' } = route.params'}</code>
        working and a default that never fires, which is a distinction an empty string would quietly
        lose.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="What the file path decides">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>src/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/invoices/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/invoices</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/invoices/[id]/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/invoices/4310</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/docs/[...path]/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/docs/start/install</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>src/billing/page.abide</code></td><td><code>/billing</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A subject's pages live with the rest of it, and a directory named <code>server</code> or
        <code>public</code> is never a segment of an address. Any other file
        beside a <code>page.abide</code> — a component, a helper — is yours, and is no address.</p>

        <p><code>route.url</code> comes from the file path and <code>route.name</code> is the
        <strong>resolution path</strong> — the pattern that matched, not the address that arrived.
        So a log line, a metric and an <code>{'{#if}'}</code> over the current route all key on
        <code>invoices/[id]</code> for every invoice rather than on one address each.</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/invoices/[id]/page.abide — excerpt" code={`<script>
import { reactive, route } from '@abide/abide'
import { getInvoice } from '#src/invoices/api.abide'

const invoice = reactive(() => getInvoice({ id: route.params.id }))
</script>

<h1>Invoice {invoice.number}</h1>
<p>{invoice.total} due {invoice.dueOn}</p>`} />

        <p>Reading <code>route.params</code> is a live read like any other, so a navigation from one
        invoice to another moves the value rather than rebuilding the page.</p>

        <p>A package can ship pages, layouts, error pages and handlers as <code>.abide</code>
        source, and the address they answer at is yours to choose. Importing one of its directories
        with <code>type: 'routes'</code> puts all of them below the file that imports it:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="abide" code={`<script module>
import 'admin-kit/admin' with { type: 'routes' }
</script>

<section class="tools"><slot/></section>`} />

        <p>Written in <code>src/tools/layout.abide</code>, that line makes
        <code>admin-kit/admin/users/[id]/page.abide</code> answer at
        <code>/tools/users/[id]</code>, inside this layout. Moving the line moves the package. Its
        files route by their path below the directory named, as yours do, and compile with your
        app on its abide. A directory mounts once, and naming one of your own directories, or one
        that is not there, fails the build.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Params` is what the segments matched">
        <p><code>Params</code> carries what <code>[param]</code>, <code>[[optional]]</code> and
        <code>[...rest]</code> matched: text, or a list for a rest. In a page it is typed from the
        page's own route rather than declared beside it — <code>abide check</code> reads
        <code>route.params()</code> in <code>invoices/[id]/page.abide</code> as
        <code>RouteParams&lt;'/invoices/[id]'&gt;</code>, so <code>id</code> is text and a key the
        route does not have is an error (23.9). A layout gets its own segments, and whatever a page
        below it adds.</p>

        <p>Nothing else is in it. A query string is not a param — it is not part of what resolved
        the route, and treating it as one is how a route starts depending on something that never
        selected it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/give-pages-the-same-chrome')}>Layouts</a> — the chrome a page
            renders inside</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/link-to-another-page')}>Links &amp; navigation</a> — building
            an address for one of these</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a> — what
            renders when a page cannot</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
